### Description

When using include_in_parent/include_in_root on nested mapping, to use parent/root level simple queries, the dense_vector fields from the nested part are also copied and cause a collision, forcing to abandon the simple queries approach on non vector fields which have its value...

searched for a way to prevent include_in_parent/include_in_root on specific fields (for example the dense_vector field) but the include_in_parent/include_in_root are a broad setting on the nested mapping level...

in my case there was no intention to copy the dense_vector to the parent/root document, and to query it with nested queries, but either not copying it or supporting multiple vector instances should do the trick

assume this mapping:
```
PUT /my_index
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "doc_title": { "type": "text" },
      "items": {
        "type": "nested",
        "include_in_parent": true,
        "properties": {
          "label": { "type": "text" },
          "item_vector": {
            "type": "dense_vector",
            "dims": 3,
            "similarity": "cosine"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

this insertion:
```
POST /my_index/_doc
{
  "doc_title": "sometitle",
  "items": [
    {"label": "aaa"},
    {"label": "bbb"}
  ]
}
```

this query works perfectly fine (using the include_in_parent/root directive)
```
POST /my_index/_search
{
  "query": {
    "match": {
      "items.label": "aaa"
    }
  }
}
```

but when multiple vector instances present, the insertion fails...
```
POST /my_index/_doc
{
  "doc_title": "sometitle",
  "items": [
    {"label": "ccc", "item_vector": [1,2,3]},
    {"label": "ddd", "item_vector": [4,5,6]}
  ]
}
```

with this error:
```
{
  "error" : {
    "root_cause" : [
      {
        "type" : "illegal_argument_exception",
        "reason" : "DocValuesField \"items.item_vector\" appears more than once in this document (only one value is allowed per field)"
      }
    ],
    "type" : "illegal_argument_exception",
    "reason" : "DocValuesField \"items.item_vector\" appears more than once in this document (only one value is allowed per field)"
  },
  "status" : 400
}
```

It'd be useful to have the rate limit field updatable so users don't have to create a new inference endpoint to modify it.

```
"rate_limit": {
