    "reason": "Cannot delete indices that are being snapshotted: [[my-index]]. Try again after snapshot finishes or cancel the currently running snapshot."
  },
  "status": 400
}
```

The issue is -- I don't care about the snapshot completing and, worse, there is a bug (that has been fixed in later versions that we are working to upgrade to) that caused snapshots to be reported as ongoing when they are infact complete (or failed). It's good that _that_ bug is fixed, but nothing stops another bug appearing in its place and causing the same issue. This can force a cluster into a scenario where they are forced to maintain gigabytes of data and, if they are using a data stream, may result in an entire data stream surviving deletion.

### Description

Lucene's `TermInSetQuery` has a fast path that skips sorting the input terms when the provided `Collection<BytesRef>` is an instance of `SortedSet` with the natural (null) comparator. Elasticsearch never takes advantage of this as it always passes a plain `BytesRef[]` array or unsorted `List`, causing Lucene to unconditionally sort the terms on every query construction.

In `TermInSetQuery.packTerms()`, Lucene checks:

```
// already sorted if we are a SortedSet with natural order
boolean sorted = terms instanceof SortedSet && ((SortedSet) terms).comparator() == null;
if (sorted == false) {
    new StringSorter(BytesRefComparator.NATURAL) { ... }.sort(0, sortedTerms.length);
}
```

However currently all call sites that construct `TermInSetQuery` in Elasticsearch pass either a raw `BytesRef[]` or a plain `List<BytesRef>` / `ArrayList`.

Recommendation:
Wherever a `Collection<BytesRef>` is assembled before being handed to `TermInSetQuery`, use a `TreeSet<BytesRef>` (which implements `SortedSet` with null natural comparator) instead:

```
// Before
List terms = new ArrayList<>();
for (Object value : values) {
    terms.add(indexedValueForSearch(value));
}
return new TermInSetQuery(name(), terms);

// After
SortedSet terms = new TreeSet<>();
for (Object value : values) {
    terms.add(indexedValueForSearch(value));
}
return new TermInSetQuery(name(), terms);  // sort bypassed by Lucene
```
Impact:
Instead of having to sort at every index-shard, we only need to sort once at the co-ordinating node and pass the SortedSet downstream. 


