### Description

Elastic Cloud would itself benefit from having the ability to dynamically set settings that themselves are not special. For example, `indices.recovery.max_bytes_per_sec` can currently be set by end users in Elastic Cloud.

But, Elastic Cloud would strongly benefit from setting this setting for end users _when it wants_ and controlling it completely. For example, if we have to vacate an allocator in Elastic Cloud, then that needs to happen as fast as reasonably possible. Elastic Cloud could check a cluster and increase its concurrent recovery settings during this window in a way that it deems safe.

The special part here is that I believe `_cluster/settings` _from Elastic Cloud_ should be set with a higher level setting that beats `transient` settings, that only Elastic Cloud (and internal admins) can set. This enables multiple things:

1. It solves any dilemma of "who set this" for everyone involved.
2. It avoids any need to worry about resetting end user settings after Elastic Cloud performs a temporary change.
3. It enables Elastic Cloud, in theoretical supported versions, to take control of dynamic settings at runtime without Elasticsearch _or_ Elastic Cloud hardcoding anything.

In essence, the idea is simple: add a new tier to `_cluster/settings` that is the highest priority tier. The name obviously is up for debate, so to make it simple I'll just call it `elastic_cloud` and leave it there:

```http
PUT /_cluster/settings
{
  "elastic_cloud": { "indices.recovery.max_bytes_per_sec": "70mb" }
}
```

```http
GET /_cluster/settings?filter_path=*.indices&include_defaults&flat_settings
{
  "elastic_cloud": {
    "indices.recovery.max_bytes_per_sec": "70mb"
  },
  "transient": {
    "indices.recovery.max_bytes_per_sec": "60mb"
  },
  "persistent": {
    "indices.recovery.max_bytes_per_sec": "50mb"
  },
  "defaults": {
    "indices.recovery.max_bytes_per_sec": "40mb"
  }
}
```

The interpreted value would be `70mb` in this example (`elastic_cloud` > `transient` > `persistent` > `defaults`). The end user could not add or remover settings from there directly and they would be treated as persistent (surviving cluster restarts).

