  "indices": [
    {
      "names": [".ml-anomalies-*"],
      "privileges": ["read"],
      "query":  {
          "terms": { 
            "job_id": ["foo"] // job ids pulled from the results of the first query 
          }
        }
    }
  ],
  ...
}
```

### Description

Restoring a cluster snapshot that still contains searchable-snapshot indices can leave those indices **red** when ILM (or anything else) has already deleted their backing snapshot. `ignore_unavailable` on the [restore snapshot API](https://www.elastic.co/docs/api/doc/elasticsearch/operation/operation-snapshot-restore) does not help: it only skips index names that are not in the snapshot. These indices **are** in the snapshot; restore recreates them, shard recovery hits `SnapshotMissingException` (e.g., missing `.dat` file), and the cluster stays red.

This shows up in particular on ECH create-from-snapshot: the constructor waits for green, the plan hangs on `wait-until-snapshot-restored` (`Indices still initializing: []`), and the deployment stays **Being Created** (and the user may not delete the red indices if REST is inaccessible). Self-managed restores have the same red leftovers, without the orchestration hang.

### Why a control plane workaround is not enough

Control plane is looking at tolerating permanently red indices while waiting for restore. That can unblock ECH access, but Elasticsearch does not expose a robust "this shard will never recover" signal, so the orchestrator has to guess (quiet-red window + allocation explain + a red-index fraction cap). A large frozen tier can still fail that cap, and the cluster is still left red.

We should handle this in restore itself so those indices are not created (or are omitted) and the restored cluster can come up without those red shards.

### Proposed direction

A restore option / whole-cluster restore mode that omits searchable-snapshot indices whose backing snapshot is gone, analogous to `ignore_unavailable` but for missing backing snapshots, not missing index names. That is one of the things a proper ["restore this whole cluster from snapshot" mode](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/98212) would need; it is also useful on its own for clone/DR of clusters that use ILM searchable snapshots.

Related docs: [Back up and restore searchable snapshots](https://www.elastic.co/docs/deploy-manage/tools/snapshot-and-restore/searchable-snapshots#back-up-restore-searchable-snapshots).

### Repro (simplified)

Here's a new test in SearchableSnapshotActionIT: ILM mounts an index as a frozen searchable snapshot (backing snapshot S1), a second repository takes a cluster snapshot S2 of that mounted index plus a regular index, then ILM delete removes the mounted index and S1. Restoring S2 with `ignore_unavailable: true` skips a name that is not in the snapshot, restores the regular index to green, and still restores the searchable-snapshot index, which stays red with `SnapshotMissingException`.

<details>
<summary>Reproduction test source code</summary>

```java
/**
 * Mirrors the ECH create-from-snapshot failure: ILM mounts an index as a searchable snapshot,
 * a later cluster snapshot captures that mounted index, then ILM deletes the index and its
 * backing snapshot. Restoring the cluster snapshot still includes the mounted index (so
 * {@code ignore_unavailable} does not skip it) and shard recovery fails with
 * {@code SnapshotMissingException}.
 */
public void testRestoreSearchableSnapshotAfterIlmDeletedBackingSnapshot() throws Exception {
    final String backupRepo = snapshotRepo + "-backup";
    final String originalIndex = "frozen-" + randomAlphaOfLength(6).toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
    final String regularIndex = "regular-" + randomAlphaOfLength(6).toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
    createSnapshotRepo(client(), snapshotRepo, randomBoolean());
    createSnapshotRepo(client(), backupRepo, randomBoolean());
    // Other tests (or a previous iteration) may have stopped ILM; make sure it is running.
    assertOK(client().performRequest(new Request("POST", "/_ilm/start")));

    createPolicy(
        client(),
        policy,
        null,
        null,
        null,
        new Phase("frozen", TimeValue.ZERO, Map.of(SearchableSnapshotAction.NAME, new SearchableSnapshotAction(snapshotRepo))),
        new Phase("delete", TimeValue.timeValueDays(1), Map.of(DeleteAction.NAME, WITH_SNAPSHOT_DELETE))
    );

    createIndex(
        originalIndex,
        Settings.builder().put(IndexMetadata.SETTING_NUMBER_OF_SHARDS, 1).put(IndexMetadata.SETTING_NUMBER_OF_REPLICAS, 0).build()
    );
    createIndex(
        regularIndex,
        Settings.builder().put(IndexMetadata.SETTING_NUMBER_OF_SHARDS, 1).put(IndexMetadata.SETTING_NUMBER_OF_REPLICAS, 0).build()
    );
    indexDocument(client(), originalIndex, true);
    indexDocument(client(), regularIndex, true);
    updateIndexSettings(originalIndex, Settings.builder().put(LifecycleSettings.LIFECYCLE_NAME, policy));

    final String mountedIndex = SearchableSnapshotAction.PARTIAL_RESTORED_INDEX_PREFIX + originalIndex;
    awaitIndexExists(mountedIndex, TimeValue.timeValueSeconds(30));
    TimeSeriesRestDriver.awaitStepKey(client(), mountedIndex, "frozen", null, PhaseCompleteStep.NAME);
    awaitIndexDoesNotExist(originalIndex);

    final String backupSnapshot = "cluster-backup";
    Request takeBackup = new Request("PUT", "/_snapshot/" + backupRepo + "/" + backupSnapshot);
    takeBackup.addParameter("wait_for_completion", "true");
    takeBackup.setJsonEntity("{\"indices\": \"" + mountedIndex + "," + regularIndex + "\", \"include_global_state\": false}");
    assertOK(client().performRequest(takeBackup));

    createPolicy(
        client(),
        policy,
        null,
        null,
        null,
        new Phase("frozen", TimeValue.ZERO, Map.of(SearchableSnapshotAction.NAME, new SearchableSnapshotAction(snapshotRepo))),
        new Phase("delete", TimeValue.ZERO, Map.of(DeleteAction.NAME, WITH_SNAPSHOT_DELETE))
    );
    awaitIndexDoesNotExist(mountedIndex, TimeValue.timeValueSeconds(30));
    assertBusy(() -> {
        List<Map<String, Object>> remaining = getSnapshots();
        assertThat(
            "backing searchable snapshot should be deleted, but got: " + remaining,
            remaining == null ? 0 : remaining.size(),
            equalTo(0)
        );
    }, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    assertOK(client().performRequest(new Request("DELETE", "/" + regularIndex)));

    Request restoreMissingIndex = new Request("POST", "/_snapshot/" + backupRepo + "/" + backupSnapshot + "/_restore");
    restoreMissingIndex.setJsonEntity("{\"indices\": \"not-in-the-snapshot\", \"ignore_unavailable\": false}");
    ResponseException missing = expectThrows(ResponseException.class, () -> client().performRequest(restoreMissingIndex));
    assertThat(missing.getMessage(), containsString("not-in-the-snapshot"));

    Request restore = new Request("POST", "/_snapshot/" + backupRepo + "/" + backupSnapshot + "/_restore");
    restore.addParameter("wait_for_completion", "true");
    restore.setJsonEntity("""
        {
          "indices": "not-in-the-snapshot,%s,%s",
          "ignore_unavailable": true,
          "include_global_state": false,
          "ignore_index_settings": [ "index.lifecycle.name" ],
          "index_settings": { "index.allocation.max_retries": 1 }
        }""".formatted(mountedIndex, regularIndex));
    assertOK(client().performRequest(restore));

    ensureGreen(regularIndex);
    awaitIndexExists(mountedIndex);
    assertBusy(() -> {
        Request explain = new Request("GET", "/_cluster/allocation/explain");
        explain.setJsonEntity("{\"index\":\"" + mountedIndex + "\",\"shard\":0,\"primary\":true}");
        assertThat(entityAsMap(client().performRequest(explain)).toString(), containsString("SnapshotMissingException"));
    }, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    Request health = new Request("GET", "/_cluster/health/" + mountedIndex);
    assertThat(ObjectPath.createFromResponse(client().performRequest(health)).evaluate("status"), equalTo("red"));
}
